<td id="A5.T6.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">65.5</td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.T6.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">37.0</span></td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">74.6</td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A5.T6.4.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">88.5</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T6.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T6.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">JustGRPO</td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.T6.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">65.8</span></td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">36.7</td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A5.T6.4.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">74.8</span></td>
<td id="A5.T6.4.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">87.5</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Note on bidirectional refinement.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because JustGRPO imposes the AR factorization only as a training-time scaffold rather than a constraint on inference, we expect it to largely preserve the bidirectional behavior characteristic of dLLMs. This expectation is consistent with the preserved parallel decoding capability reported in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 JustGRPO Preserves Parallel Decoding ‣ 5 Experiments" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
A natural extension is to ask whether dLLMs can iteratively refine and revise their already-decoded outputs. This connects to corrective approaches such as CDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> and to the bidirectional-context settings studied in ParallelBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al. (2026)</a></cite>, and we view it as a promising direction that could complement JustGRPO.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section><div class="ltx_rdf" about="" property="dcterms:title" content="The Flexibility Trap: Rethinking the Value of Arbitrary Order in Diffusion Language Models"></div>

</article>
</div>
</div>
